{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"06","chapter_title":"Data Storytelling With Excel","title":"Data Ethics in Small Datasets","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting data ethics in small datasets to Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-01","statement":"Identify when a small dataset is re-identifiable through a combination of quasi-identifiers even after direct identifiers are removed"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-02","statement":"Apply a three-point ethics screen (re-identification, group-of-one, honest magnitude) to decide how to report a small-n finding"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-04","statement":"Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel","cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/03/05"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_calibration","cat100-supplement_03_06_lo_03_direct_check"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_artifact_signal"],"cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-03":["cat100-supplement_03_06_lo_03_direct_check"],"cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_ai_boundary"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Data Ethics in Small Datasets\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Data Ethics In Small Datasets, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/03-06-data-ethics-in-small-datasets.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Data Ethics in Small Datasets: Data Ethics In Small Datasets, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\nWhen a dataset has thousands of rows, one person's record disappears into the crowd. When it has seven rows, every record is a recognizable individual, and the ethical stakes of how you handle and report it rise sharply. A small dataset is the most common kind a student actually works with, a club survey, a class poll, a team roster, and it is precisely where privacy and honesty are easiest to violate by accident.\n\nThis section teaches two related disciplines for small data. The first is protecting identity: removing a name is not the same as anonymizing, because a rare combination of attributes can still point to one person. The second is reporting honestly: a percentage over seven people, or an average over a group of one, can mislead a reader into seeing a pattern that is really just noise. Both are decisions you make at the spreadsheet, and both protect real people.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Identify when a small dataset is re-identifiable through a combination of quasi-identifiers even after direct identifiers are removed.\n- Apply a three-point ethics screen (re-identification, group-of-one, honest magnitude) to decide how to report a small-n finding.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nYou survey seven members of a campus club and want to share the results. You delete the name column, feeling responsible, and report that \"57% chose option A.\" Both moves feel careful. Both are quietly unsafe. The row for the only computer-science major living in Hall C still identifies that one person even with the name gone, and \"57%\" makes seven opinions sound like a representative measurement.\n\nThese are not exotic mistakes; they are the default outcome of treating a small dataset like a big one. The work of this section is to slow down at exactly these moments, to recognize when a \"deidentified\" row is still re-identifiable and when a tidy percentage is hiding a tiny, fragile sample.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nTwo ideas do the heavy lifting here. The first is re-identification through quasi-identifiers. A direct identifier (a name, an ID number) names someone outright, but a quasi-identifier is any attribute that, combined with others, narrows the field to one person: major, dorm, age, hometown. In a large dataset many people share each combination; in a seven-row table a single rare combination can be unique. So anonymizing means looking at combinations of columns, not just deleting the name.\n\n**Recent evidence:** this is a documented finding, not just instructor caution. Latanya Sweeney showed that about 87% of the US population (216 of 248 million people, using 1990 Census data) were likely uniquely identifiable from just three quasi-identifiers: 5-digit ZIP code, gender, and date of birth (Sweeney, 2000). Her follow-up work formalized the protection as k-anonymity: a released record should be indistinguishable from at least k-1 other records on its quasi-identifiers (Sweeney, 2002). In a 7-row table the mechanism is even sharper, because almost any combination of two columns can be unique.\n\nThe second idea is honest reporting at small n (the number of observations). At small n, two habits protect the reader:\n\n| Practice | Why it matters at small n |\n| --- | --- |\n| Report raw counts, not just percentages | \"4 of 7\" is honest; \"57%\" implies a precision the sample lacks |\n| Name the sample size and that it is not representative | Seven club members do not speak for the campus |\n| Never publish a statistic over a group of one | An average of one both exposes the person and fakes a pattern |\n| Suppress or merge thin categories | A subgroup with n of 1 should be combined, not reported alone |\n\nThe throughline is that small numbers demand both more privacy care and more humility in what you claim, because each row is a person and each subgroup may be a single voice.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nSuppose you survey 7 club members and start with this sheet.\n\n```\n   A        B     C        D            E\n1  Name     Age   Major    Dorm         Pick\n2  removed  19    Nursing  Hall B       A\n3  removed  20    Biology  Hall A       A\n4  removed  19    History  Off-campus   B\n5  removed  21    Nursing  Hall B       A\n6  removed  22    CompSci  Hall C       A\n7  removed  20    Biology  Hall A       B\n8  removed  19    Art      Hall B       B\n```\n\nStep 1. The name column is blank, but row 6 is still re-identifiable: only one CompSci major lives in Hall C. Deleting the name did not make the dataset anonymous. Combine or generalize the major and dorm columns (for example, group dorms into 'on-campus' vs 'off-campus') so no single combination points to one person.\n\nStep 2. Report the headline honestly. 4 of 7 picked A. Writing '57% chose A' hides that the whole survey was 7 people. The honest statement is '4 of 7 respondents (a small, non-representative sample) chose A'.\n\nStep 3. If you split 'Pick' by Major, several majors have an n of 1. An average or percentage over a group of one both exposes that individual and pretends a single answer is a pattern. Suppress those cells or merge the categories.\n\nThe auditable decision: anonymize by combinations not just the name, report raw counts on small n, and never publish a group of one. Each step protects a real person in a 7-row table.\n\nWalk the same 7-row club survey and decide each protective move before you reveal it.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Make the 7-row club survey safe to share\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You already deleted the Name column. Row 6 is the only CompSci major in Hall C. Is the table now anonymous?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, removing the name is enough\",\"No, the Major plus Dorm combination still singles out one person\",\"Yes, because nobody else shares row 6 exactly\",\"No, but only if someone has the original sheet\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Deleting the direct identifier does not anonymize. The quasi-identifier combination (CompSci plus Hall C) is unique in 7 rows, so generalize: group dorms into on-campus vs off-campus, or collapse Major into a broad field.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You want to publish the headline. 4 of 7 picked A. Which wording is honest at this sample size?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"57% chose A\",\"A majority chose A\",\"4 of 7 respondents (a small, non-representative sample) chose A\",\"Most members support A\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"Report the raw count and name the n. 57% implies a precision seven opinions cannot carry; 4 of 7 with the caveat tells the reader exactly how thin the evidence is.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You split Pick by Major and several majors have n of 1. What do you do with those cells?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Report each one so the table is complete\",\"Suppress or merge the thin categories\",\"Convert them to percentages to hide the count\",\"Average them together with the larger groups silently\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A value over a group of one both exposes that individual and fakes a pattern. Suppress the cell or merge the category before anything is published.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The finished move: generalize quasi-identifiers so no row is unique, report 4 of 7 with the sample size named, and suppress every group of one. The 7-row table is now both anonymous and honestly framed.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nNow order the ethics screen a reviewer would run on any small dataset, from layout-level identity protection to the final honesty check.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Delete direct identifiers (names, ID numbers)\",\"Scan combinations of remaining columns for any unique row, then generalize or merge\",\"Suppress or combine any subgroup with n of 1\",\"Report raw counts and name the sample size instead of a bare percentage\",\"State plainly that the sample is small and not representative\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nProtect the person and respect the sample size. Before you share any small-dataset finding, check that no row is uniquely identifiable, no group of one is reported, and no percentage hides how few people it represents.\n\nApply this three-point ethics screen to a small dataset:\n\n1. **Re-identification check:** Could any combination of remaining columns single out one individual? If so, generalize or merge those columns.\n2. **Group-of-one check:** Does any reported average or percentage cover a subgroup with n of 1? If so, suppress or combine it. A common rule of thumb in education reporting is to suppress or merge any subgroup smaller than about 5 (NCES guidance; some agencies, like CMS, use stricter thresholds), and in a 7-row table that means almost everything must be reported in aggregate. It is a guideline rather than a law, and it tightens as the attribute gets more sensitive.\n3. **Honest-magnitude check:** Are you reporting raw counts and naming the sample size, rather than a bare percentage that implies precision?\n\nIf a friend in the dataset could recognize their own row from your published summary, the re-identification check failed.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most consequential misreading is \"I deleted the names, so it's anonymous.\" Deletion removes the direct identifier but leaves the quasi-identifiers untouched, and in a seven-row table the lone nursing major in Hall B is as identifiable as if you had printed her name. Anonymizing means flattening rare combinations, often by grouping dorms into on- and off-campus or collapsing majors into broad fields.\n\nA second misreading is preferring percentages because they look professional. \"57% chose A\" sounds like a poll result; \"4 of 7 respondents chose A\" tells the truth that the whole study was seven people. At small n the raw count is the honest form, and dropping the sample size from the sentence is itself a small deception.\n\n<inline-check question=\"You publish a six-person internship summary with the Name column deleted. A reader recognizes one row as their friend, the only intern from the marketing department. Which protection failed?\" explanation=\"A direct identifier was removed, but a quasi-identifier (the lone marketing department) still singles out one person. That is the re-identification check failing, not a reporting or sample-size error. The fix is to generalize or aggregate Department rather than leaving the unique value in place.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The honest-magnitude check, because a percentage was used instead of a count\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The re-identification check, because a unique quasi-identifier still points to one person\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Nothing failed; deleting the name column makes the data anonymous\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the screen to a different small dataset: a six-person internship cohort's exit feedback, with columns Name, Department, Hometown, and a 1-to-5 satisfaction score. You want to publish a short summary for next year's applicants.\n\nStart with re-identification. Delete Name, then look at the survivors: only one intern came from the marketing department, and only one listed a small hometown. Either column alone re-identifies someone, so generalize Hometown to a region and report Department only in aggregate, not row by row. Next, the group-of-one check: if you split satisfaction by department, marketing has n of 1, so reporting \"marketing scored 2/5\" both exposes that intern and treats one opinion as a department verdict. Merge departments into a single overall figure instead.\n\nFinally, honest magnitude. Rather than \"67% were satisfied,\" write \"4 of 6 interns rated their experience 4 or higher (small cohort, not representative).\" The summary now informs applicants without exposing any single intern or overstating six data points.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of a small-data report instinctively asks who could be identified and how fragile the numbers are. Train these reflexes:\n\n1. Scan for any subgroup small enough that one person defines it, and treat a reported value over that group as a red flag.\n2. Mentally reconstruct whether a known individual's row could be picked out from the published attributes.\n3. Convert every percentage back to a count over the stated n, and notice when the n is missing.\n\nWhen a clean-looking \"73% agree\" makes you immediately ask \"out of how many?\", you are reading the ethics, not just the result.\n\nThese reading reflexes connect the section concept to the module-wide habits of evidence-based revision and bounded AI support. Trace how they relate.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets\", \"cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong small-data artifact documents the privacy and honesty decisions, not just the polished chart. The trace should name what you generalized to prevent re-identification, which thin groups you suppressed, and why you reported counts with the sample size. A model line reads: \"I grouped dorms into on- and off-campus because the Hall C plus computer-science combination identified one member; I reported '4 of 7 chose A' rather than '57%' so no reader overreads a seven-person poll.\"\n\nThe weak version shares a tidy percentage and a deidentified-looking table. The strong version makes the protective choices visible, so a reviewer can confirm that no individual is exposed and no claim outruns the sample.\n\n**Why the screen is not optional.** When the small dataset holds other students' records, FERPA applies: personally identifiable information includes not just direct identifiers but indirect identifiers and \"other information that, alone or in combination, is linkable to a specific student\" (U.S. Department of Education, studentprivacy.ed.gov). The quasi-identifier risk this section teaches is the federal definition, not an abstraction.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe same ethics screen transfers from a survey to an open-ended dataset, where the risk hides in the text. Suppose you collect short written comments from eight tutoring clients to share themes. There is no name column, but a comment that mentions a specific course, a specific time slot, and a personal detail can identify the writer as surely as a name. The re-identification check now means scanning quotes for identifying specifics and paraphrasing them, and the group-of-one check means not attributing a unique, recognizable comment to its lone author. The data type changed from numbers to text, but the duty, prevent re-identification and do not over-claim from few voices, is the same, which shows the screen is about the people, not the cell format.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/03-06-data-ethics-in-small-datasets-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"03.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title for CAT 100 03.06. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- U.S. Department of Education, Protecting Student Privacy: Personally identifiable information and data de-identification terms. https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/content/personally-identifiable-information-education-records\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n- Sweeney, L. (2000). Simple demographics often identify people uniquely (Data Privacy Working Paper 3). Carnegie Mellon University. https://dataprivacylab.org/projects/identifiability/paper1.pdf\n- Sweeney, L. (2002). k-anonymity: A model for protecting privacy. International Journal of Uncertainty, Fuzziness and Knowledge-Based Systems, 10(5), 557-570. https://doi.org/10.1142/S0218488502001648\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets","cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel"],"stem":"A club survey has 8 respondents. One row reads: 'Age 19, Major Nursing, Dorm Hall B, Works at campus library front desk'. Why is this row a privacy concern even without a name column?","options":["It is fine because the name was removed.","The combination of major, dorm, and job is specific enough to re-identify one person in a small group.","It is a concern only if an email address is present.","Eight rows is too few to ever be a privacy concern."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Removing the name is not enough. A combination of quasi-identifiers (major, dorm, specific job) can single out one person, especially in a small dataset.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_anonymous_if_no_name","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets"],"stem":"Your dataset has 6 students. You make a chart titled 'Average GPA by gender' where one group has only 1 person. What is the ethical problem with publishing that bar?","options":["The bar will be the wrong color.","An average over a group of 1 both exposes that individual's exact value and presents a single point as a reliable group pattern.","Averages cannot be computed for one person.","The chart should use a pie chart instead."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A group of one is not anonymous and not a pattern. The 'average' is just that person's value, and presenting it as a group trend is both a privacy and an honesty problem. Suppress or combine tiny groups.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_small_n_as_pattern","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets","ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"You want an AI tool to help summarize a small dataset that contains classmates' names and email addresses. What is the responsible step before pasting it in?","options":["Paste the whole sheet because the tool is private.","Remove or mask the direct identifiers (names, emails) first, since you should not share others' personal data with a third-party tool.","Paste only the names so the tool can label rows.","Add more personal detail so the summary is accurate."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Sharing classmates' names and emails with an external tool exposes their data without consent. 'The tool is private' is not a safe assumption: as of 2026, consumer AI chat tools (such as the free or personal tiers of ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini) may retain pasted text and use it for model training by default unless you opt out in settings, while enterprise or institution-licensed tiers typically do not train on submitted content by default. Strip or mask direct identifiers before pasting, regardless of which tool or tier you use.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_sharing_identifiers","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_data_ethics_in_small_datasets","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"From a survey of 7 people, 4 chose option A. Which reporting choice is the most honest?","options":["Report '57% of respondents chose A' with no mention of the sample size.","Report '4 of 7 respondents chose A' so the reader sees how small the sample is.","Report 'a majority of people prefer A' as a general claim.","Report 'most users choose A' to sound more confident."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"With a tiny sample, a raw count like '4 of 7' is more honest than a percentage that hides how few responses there were. Percentages on small n can mislead.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_small_n_as_pattern","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100-supplement_03_06_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-06-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_anonymous_if_no_name","pattern":"anonymous_once_name_removed","description":"Believing data is anonymous as soon as the name column is deleted, ignoring that combined quasi-identifiers can re-identify a person","trigger":"learner calls a small dataset anonymous after removing only the name","feedback":"Removing the name is not enough. A combination of major, dorm, role, or other details can re-identify one person in a small group.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_small_n_as_pattern","pattern":"small_n_treated_as_pattern","description":"Presenting averages or percentages from a tiny sample as if they were stable group patterns, hiding how few responses there were","trigger":"learner reports a percentage over a handful of rows without showing the count","feedback":"With a tiny sample, show raw counts like '4 of 7' and suppress or combine groups of one. A small n is not a reliable pattern.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_6_data_ethics_in_small_datasets_sharing_identifiers","pattern":"sharing_others_identifiers","description":"Pasting other people's names, emails, or direct identifiers into an external tool without consent or masking","trigger":"learner shares a dataset of classmates' personal data with a third-party tool","feedback":"Remove or mask direct identifiers before using any external tool. You should not expose others' personal data without consent.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"5ec58d16a46db4f24821823fe4ae65f7e7788ab2296f573b2c58e908123147a9","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}